  "title": "S1b cache_telemetry: per-invocation prompt-cache economics (hit ratio, effective cost, prefix identity) from existing run rows and packet metadata (P6)",
  "spec": "Create orchestrator/cache_telemetry.py. normalize(provider, usage) -> dict(input_uncached, cache_read, cache_write, output, hit_ratio) where hit_ratio = cache_read / (input_uncached + cache_read + cache_write) or 0.0; Claude usage keys input_tokens, cache_read_input_tokens, cache_creation_input_tokens, output_tokens; Codex keys input_tokens (includes cached), cached_input_tokens, output_tokens (see bus.py usage normalization around line 224 and executor.py around line 113; reuse, do not duplicate the semantics). effective_cost(norm, cfg) = input_uncached + cache_read*read_ratio + cache_write*write_ratio + output*output_ratio in token-equivalents, with per-provider ratios from pool.toml [cache] (new section: claude_read_ratio default 0.1, claude_write_ratio 1.25, codex_read_ratio 0.25, codex_write_ratio 1.0, output_ratio 5.0; add the section with dated comments).\nPrefix identity, defined on the final prompt: spawn.render (spawn.py near line 242) assembles the role template text, composed instruction modules and tool disclosure text around the packet placeholder; the packet itself begins with a header line 'packet v<hash> ...' whose hash covers the whole task-specific body. Prefix = everything render() emits BEFORE that packet header line (template rules, modules, tool disclosure); suffix = the packet (header and all sections). render() computes prefix_sha (sha256, first 12 hex), prefix_chars, suffix_chars and dynamic_sections (the packet section names) and records them on the task's packet_meta through _remember_packet_meta keyed by the packet version, and bus.update(task_id, packet_meta=...) where the task id is known; packet_meta(task, worktree) itself is unchanged apart from carrying those keys when present. Consequence: two tasks of the same role whose templates, modules and disclosed tools are identical share prefix_sha even though their packets differ. Do not reorder or change any section content in this task (stage 2 does). Also make bus.log_run rows (already written by spawn/executor with usage) carry cache fields: input_uncached_tokens, cache_read_tokens, cache_write_tokens, hit_ratio, effective_tokens, prefix_sha when available, via a single helper cache_telemetry.annotate(row) called from bus.log_run (guard: never raise from log_run).\nreport(root, days=7) -> per role and per provider: runs, input_uncached, cache_read, cache_write, hit_ratio, effective_tokens, distinct prefix_sha count and the share of runs whose prefix_sha was seen before within the window (reuse_rate); plus per goal totals. CLI: `orchestrator scorecard --cache [--days n] [--json]` (extend the existing scorecard subcommand in cli.py). The report must run on this repository's real .orchestrator/runs/*.jsonl rows (they exist for 2026-09-17..23; rows before this task lack prefix_sha and count as unknown prefix). Docs: docs/hermes-hardening/02-cache-telemetry.md with the definitions and the numbers measured on the real rows.\nSpec review T-1028 folded in: the prefix definition above replaces both earlier wordings (the packet header line is task-specific and belongs to the suffix; the hash is computed in render(), which is the only place the full prompt exists).\nSpec review T-0999 folded in: (1) Prefix definition: prefix = the rendered text strictly BEFORE the objective section (role template rules plus the skills section when present); the objective section and everything after it is the suffix. prefix_sha must be identical for two tasks of the same role and goal that differ only in task-specific sections. (2) bus.log_run already writes input_uncached_tokens, cache_read_tokens, cache_write_tokens and output_tokens through normalize_usage (bus.py near line 353): cache_telemetry.normalize is a thin wrapper that returns exactly those key names (with the _tokens suffix) by calling bus.normalize_usage, and annotate(row) reuses the row's existing fields, adding only hit_ratio, effective_tokens and prefix_sha (from the task's packet_meta when present); never a second divergent key set. (3) cli.py scorecard: --cache joins the existing mutual-exclusivity check of scorecard modes (near line 216) as one more exclusive mode. (4) Task worktrees have no .orchestrator/runs data (uncommitted state): tests use synthetic rows only; the report function takes root explicitly and the CLI accepts --root; for the numbers in docs/hermes-hardening/02-cache-telemetry.md run the report read-only against /Users/k3ntaw/code/orchestrator/.orchestrator (the main checkout) and state that rows before this task carry no prefix_sha, so the report shows the unknown-prefix share and reuse_rate is reported as not measurable until new rows arrive (no invented baseline).\nRepository rules: unittest (not pytest), tests/_harness.py single ORCH_ROOT fixture; every acceptance test id tests/test_<module>.py::test_name is a method def test_name(self) in a unittest.TestCase. No new third-party dependencies. Never write secret values to any file or log. Do not quote template placeholder tokens (double braces) anywhere in code comments or docs. Run .claude/hooks/tests-green.sh . until green; the base suite must stay green. Do not ask questions: decide, and note decisions in the result summary. Read docs/context-economy/00-overview.md only if you need the evidence/decision_log conventions.",
